But if we want to talk  "realism", peaceful and hostile refer to monsters' 
attitudes to the *player*, not the pet. Why should throwing a fortune 
cookie at a cat cause it to consider (or even be able to discern) the 
attitude of another monster to you before deciding whether to attack it? 
You certainly haven't had time for training the pet to voice commands etc. 
Isn't it at least as "realistic" to rationalise current behaviour as being 
that the pet recognises the dungeons of doom as a hostile environment? Thus 
to the pet, all its inhabitants are hostile and any monster that the pet 
thinks it can beat should be attacked.

For that matter, why don't hostile monsters initiate attacks on pets? If 
the pet is 2 or more levels lower than the monster, both leave each other 
alone. How "realistic" is that?

And why don't shopkeepers have "no pets" signs and attack them and/or 
demand compensation for goods eaten or taken?

