As NHIND&D, and additionally AFAIK, this is not mandated by the D&D rules 
but is simply your interpretation of them, this is a weak argument. Also, 
who controls the actions of the NPCs when you are DM? If the players 
control the actions directly ("my henchman chops the fluffy bunny into 
little pieces") then what you do is reasonable. If the players strictly 
control only their own characters ("I tell my henchman it's none of my 
business whether he chops the fluffy bunny into little pieces") then it is 
not reasonable; they should be penalised for their own players' actions. 
Current nethack behaviour is closer to the second case.

If you really do want to penalise players for their pets' actions, then the 
suggested change won't help at all, as all you are doing is changing the 
rules that govern pet behaviour; it doesn't give the player any more 
control over their actions or any more interest in their consequences. If 
that's your aim, it would be better to make pets' actions affect alignment 
etc. in the same way as the player's - your pet kills a shopkeeper gives 
"you murderer!" with the usual consequences; your monk's pet eats a meaty 
corpse - goodbye vegetarian conduct and -1 to alignment; your knight's pet 
attacks an incapacitated monster - "you caitiff!"...

